terests for example  scholars note that  much of the crime related news that had  previously dominated the peacetime press  was steadily eliminated to make space  for an important new emphasis stories of  black troops and their role in the  services newspapers also brought in more  columnist representing an enormous range  of interests and philosophies from  conservative too radical these strategic  changes were a coordinated effort aimed  at not only stimulating sales but also  to give to giving voice to emerging  audiences that were increasingly urban  literate and militant the centrality of  the African American soldier within  these cartoons is not surprising as  David cario writes the meaning and  content of cartoons and comics quote is  determined not by the artist but by the  audience reflecting the fantasies of its  public end quote the soldier was an  effective public image for pointing to  the country's military it's fight for  democracy and in this case its racial  iniquities in these cartoons men and  women were assigned with almost endless  potential encouraged by the belief that  each of them could disrupt assume  notions of inferiority rampant with in  every sector of the war industry at the  time the soldier image mediated a desire  for a new afro-american African American  hero doing so by accomplishing three  tasks first it allowed cartoonists to  render a variety of race issues on an  individual scale making them more  readable and giving voice to those  people oppressed by them second it  presented a dual image at once positive  patriotic cleverly protesting  institutionalized discrimination in the  military and engaging otherwise lukewarm  attitudes that many african-americans  had for the war and third it functioned  to advance representation the  representation of African Americans  instilling a self-image as a modern  soldier or modern American replacing  derogatory stereotypical characters by  whites from decades past a cartoon in  the Balti