ernment's ability to deny  training J Jackson wilbert Holloway  Francis Yancy George Mercer Sims  Campbell Oliver Harrington and many  cartoonists working for the press at  this time consciously selected  structured and captioned their soldier  in this soldier image doing so with  details that in truth rose above the  message of the editorials their work  both in cartoons and comic strips  expanded the possibilities of cartoon  imagery more generally to combat  negative stereotyping of African  Americans Oliver Harrington who  contributed comic strips and was likely  the cartoonists behind some of the  African afro-american cartoons seen here  a test to specifically defining his  figures in reaction to the draw  tori imagery of decades earlier like the  examples seen here he stated quote black  faces were always represented as a  circle black with two hot dogs in the  middle for a mouth the black figure and  my cartooning it was a conscious effort  on my part to change that the black had  to disappear the rubber lips had to  disappear end quote Harrington's urgent  need to counteract negative imagery and  eradicate the black and the lips stems  from what Michael Harris identifies as a  conceptual and corporate incarceration  he writes quote the derogatory and  servitor typical imagery found in 19th  century minstrel performance and popular  media combined with political social and  economic oppression and the official and  renegade enforcement of racial ideology  to control and trap the black individual  within his or her community and  ultimately within his or her body and  quote Harrington Jackson and others  clearly utilized their cartoons to break  free of this control in the page of the  African American press there is a rather  consistent focus on the figure  aggressively pinpointing and acting and  acting against this history Harrington  in particular states that he turned to  the work of Milton Kenneth at this time  whose action-adventure comic strip Terry  