and the Pirates set new standards of  comic art with its authentic highly  detailed backgrounds and dramatically  stage figures the effort to reinvent to  re-image the figure was not unique to  cartoonists though by 1940 large  circulation newspapers like the  afro-american the defender and the  Pittsburgh courier shared images of and  information about african-americans for  african-americans in a variety of  positive ways advertisements for example  guided readers through society  suggesting where to shop with without  humiliation or where to apply for a job  that would not discriminate newspapers  also included sports and society pages  praising athletes and professionals  otherwise ignored by the white press  many of the larger papers also formally  organized in 1940 creating the National  Negro publishers association or NNPA a  vitally important coalition of editors  the nnpa facilitated a you know  viewpoint and coordinated policies among  the country's largest papers within  three months after the war the start of  World War two they also adopted a war  resolution pledging to collectively  campaigned for victory at home and  abroad this this morphed into unique yet  aligned campaigns the double V campaign  initiated by the Pittsburgh courier and  the Baltimore afro-american and the  defenders remember Pearl Harbor and  sikeston to generating national debate  they advocated participation in the war  and an equal protest against racism and  discrimination on the homefront the  Chicago defenders campaign linked the  Pearl Harbor attack with a horrific  lynching in Sikeston Missouri that  occurred in January 1942 itself a  recurrent theme in the wartime press  well aware that these campaigns would  unquestionably foretell clashes with the  government the coalition of the nnpa  offered each newspaper a level of  confidence through a vast organizational  network of partnerships colonists of the  white press immediately labeled the  Black Press and organ of protest black  editor is in turn reverted the white  response to their audience saying quote  to the Negro and his press alike the  efforts of a large section of white  America to check his protest rather than  examine and act upon its causes seemed  utterly at variance with the avowed  Democratic objectives of the present  world struggle end quote those  skillfully designed to spur debate such  statements agitated government agencies  one historian writes quote it became  increasingly clear to many editors just  how seriously their position with the  government had deteriorated when scare  words such as suppression and sedition  charges began to filter into the  newspaper offices end quote six  different agencies from the FBI to the  post office began investigations into  various African American newspapers in  early nineteen forty two editors of the  Black Press and civic leaders were asked  to meet with the FBI about the  popularity of their campaigns and the  threat to african-american morale those  some scholars note that these meetings  only solidified the racial militancy of  the press others reveal a more positive  outcome historians Charles Simmons and  Patrick Washburn refer to these meetings  as actually for stalling more excessive  levels of militancy Simmons writes quote  despite the militant front originally  presented by editors government  officials were so persuasive with their  talks that some editors decided to take  another look at their editorial  philosophies and agreed to approach new  opinions with some caution end quote  while it is unclear which editors soften  their stance or how their influence made  its way through the NNPA aspects of this  modified approach seem to inflict the  cartoons that followed innate and  notable ways within the first three  issues of the Chicago Defender in March  oops the wrong way within the first  three issues of the Chicago Defender in  march of 1942 we see action no longer  dwelling on giving blood or insisting on  training the new African American  soldier here exemplifies engagement  readiness and loyalty in alienating the  affections of his strongest ally in the  upper-left corner for example a hulking  oversized african-american figure  representing the combined strength of  the world's colored races and sympathies  rises up breaking is changed with chains  with a clenched fist as he turns his  attention to the miniscule race baiter  brandished brandishing a sword in his  opposite hand behind the massive form  another tiny avatar of Nazism quips I  hope they never get together and team up  team up on me and the defenders so sorry  please at the bottom from a week later a  black GI newly arrived in Australia  grits his teeth and crouches next to a  sign proclaiming no colored races  allowed his grimace and tight grip on  the trigger a signal of fierce anger and  defiance that appears as directed at the  sign as it is on on it's not the enemy  even so he appears to trudge on to  battle  the following weeks cartoon she  delivered me and I will deliver her on  the upper right shows lady democracy  under siege her home in the background  now in flames democracy is held in a  stranglehold by a wide-eyed Nazi Nazi  officer glowering as he shouts loose me  a stern powerful african-american man  rolls up his sleeve about to wallop the  forces of rage race prejudice that keep  him from saving her a short essay  accompanies this scene with the ending  quote mr. president if you have an  answer for Japan Italy in Germany here  are those who will speed it to them  speed it in tracks of blood with hearts  as loyal as unyielding faith here they  are mr. president ready eager loyal true  as the stars end quote featuring the  heightened drama of action adventure  comic strips the cartons the cartoon  seems to unleash the African American  soldier portraying him as the only hope  for democracy presenting action loyalty  and change other subsequent cartoons  suggest that women complete this hopeful  hopeful patriotic galvanizing image too  invoking notables Sojourner Truth and  Harriet Beecher Stowe the image on the  right carries an editorial with the  decrees quote so they are ready eager  for Wars alarms its suffering and  sacrifices ready or than thousands of  brothers as ready as any women called by  the Union to the colors of freedom end  quote by contrast the cartoon on the  Left offers Frank comment on the  politically engaged domestic burdened  with the mundane others like back to  defense makes dinging attacks on the  unfortunate politics behind the Women's  Auxiliary corps that undermined efforts  to attract african-american recruits the  recruits themselves are model citizens  though loyal to the flag and insistently  walking past barriers to return to David  carriers phrase we see fantasies of the  public emerging here as a quick survey  of later cartoons from 1942 illustrate  whether acknowledging new policy changes  questioning the very nature of victory  or dramatizing the historical moment  through comedic trope  the soldier humanized abstract comments  at work within Jim Crow society and the  government military complex he or she  also iterated a mental image for  millions of readers of what action and  loyalty looked like forming positive  encouraging models that function to spur  debate boost morale and temper excessive  militancy cartoons like these reflect  the complexity of the african-american  experience during World War two they  alternate between a true hero or shining  example and its opposite lashing out  against preconceived notions of  patriotism yet also enriching them the  new soldiers here are historical  correctives disruptive agents of voice  in action representing unique amalgams  of loyalty to American democracy in  protest and protest against the morally  wrong elements within it in truth both  sides of the subtle dualism remained  crucial cartoonists had to appease both  conservative and militant factions and  reassure government agencies that they  would keep up morale for the war by  keeping the public fantasy of an African  American soldier fresh in the minds of  readers week after week cartoonists did  both humor is a historical cultural  product changing as social mores and  lifestyle shift to keep up with a modern  world as a public locus of humor these  cartoons helped breeders identify with a  new image a modern soldier and mediate  strong feelings about the needs fears  and identities identity of communities  nationwide each cartoon thus occupies a  somewhat reflexive function one built  off of reader response filtered by an  editor and interpreted by an artist from  source to message to cultural product as  we begin to reconstruct the humor of  this era the emergence of this hero  confirms new elements of the  african-american experience fighting for  country fighting for rights turning away  from the past and fighting for a better  future thank  you
 

